#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133469 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ebe5738-7682-55ad-a21b-235063f08b88" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Period of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "9a8dfc78-929c-525b-9403-7c1d11d8713f" :question/math-academy-id "e-1124" :question/problem "What is the period of the function shown below?\n\n![](images/07/0780a6b1bd6f6d738c9e80e13757ff24d688728aaf8ac306284761b0e1124764.png)" :question/worked-solution "Note that if we shift the graph $3$ units left or right, the graph remains unchanged.\n\nOn the other hand, if we shift the graph horizontally by any distance other than $3$ (or a multiple other than $3$), we get a different graph.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133470 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57b95b5b-8834-5d1a-8380-fd8889e05cb4" :knowledge-point/title "Drawing the Graph of a Periodic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1529ba15-ecf2-5c3f-871d-ab249ef9af34" :question/math-academy-id "e-1125" :question/problem "Part of the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ with period $2$ is shown below. What is the graph of $y=f(x)$ on the interval $-4\\leq x \\leq 4?$\n\n![](images/59/59abf18dc978f48da4bec95bc702141b2459a327f194fda46d64f12c5bc8ffb4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The graph above shows the function in the interval $0 \\leq x \\leq 2.$\n\nSince this interval has length $2,$ and the period of the function is also $2,$ this interval represents a single period of the function.\n\nTherefore, the function will consist of many copies of the shape shown above, shifted horizontally by multiples of $2.$\n\n![](images/41/41a583651bf690810335776b9e7b5620e6b6830e1b59ee9d646c4ab429e17de8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "282b873c-1ba0-5205-b75f-c58721ffaaff" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Values of a Periodic Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "6f991ce1-e19a-5324-830a-a17f7ac22848" :question/math-academy-id "e-1126" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Given that $f$ is periodic with period $4$, what is the value of $f(84)?$\n\n![](images/b7/b763649968dc1a117007eba0b7cb0862473aadf702e8c91fc30ac83149445865.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since the period is $4,$ if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $4$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $4$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(84) & =f(84-4) \\\\  & =f(84-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =⋯ \\\\  & =f(84-20\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =f(4) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "282b873c-1ba0-5205-b75f-c58721ffaaff" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Values of a Periodic Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22df6560-8139-5a26-bc59-34b3534c24dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551744} {:answer/id #uuid "291be3f1-6ab3-5ca2-969d-d35982157ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551758} {:answer/id #uuid "8273c777-db67-55cd-bdfb-8dce1c8dd323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551974} {:answer/id #uuid "976a2557-4408-5355-9ceb-616d964de36f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552030} {:answer/id #uuid "c537f569-38b9-5883-912c-b604eeb7090f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8273c777-db67-55cd-bdfb-8dce1c8dd323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551974} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aa0efd5-2500-5a96-9f90-880911e992ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e32adf3-1916-555c-a43d-93229e1ec567" :question/math-academy-id "q-165279" :question/problem "![](images/93/93d069bfebd0d91cac459285a0e92cd14b5eab90bc465d7728b80b4f6fcbd47a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given that $y=f(x)$ is periodic with period $5,$ what is the value of $f(-37)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the period is $5,$ if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $5$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep adding $5$ to the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-37) & =f(-37+5) \\\\  & =f(-37+2\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =⋯ \\\\  & =f(-37+8\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =f(3) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ebe5738-7682-55ad-a21b-235063f08b88" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Period of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06513a58-4cc0-5b81-bebc-2d10358943b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551678} {:answer/id #uuid "097f4aa6-095b-5a90-87ea-f7eda655a28f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551685} {:answer/id #uuid "39811674-c697-5ed1-a214-03b6ca9591c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551801} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6b864f-6314-5fbf-9f53-462a942488f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552085} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8def1c-4a36-5c20-a695-b09fcfab5a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06513a58-4cc0-5b81-bebc-2d10358943b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551678} :answer-field/id #uuid "70503a3f-6957-5f30-93a0-7c9d43646755" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46b9cd99-164f-5948-814f-d29d48209d6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-41412" :question/problem "![](images/36/36e665c9de31f820a3ce60caea7f596198341cae67a60029a850f7a0a3403574.png)\n\nThe graph of a periodic function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. What is the period of this function?" :question/worked-solution "Note that if we shift the graph $2$ units left or right, the graph remains unchanged.\n\nOn the other hand, if we shift the graph horizontally by any distance other than $2$ (or a multiple of $2$), we get a different graph.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ebe5738-7682-55ad-a21b-235063f08b88" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Period of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb1cd0e-403b-579f-bb9a-3247054d5fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551697} {:answer/id #u